## 31. Plain-English Summary

This section is only a convenience summary. The full License controls if there is any conflict.

**This summary describes what applies when this License is the license actually governing a given Project.** If a Project is instead explicitly released under a different license (for example, MIT), that other license governs instead — see Section 3. A LaLibertia Project being publicly visible does not by itself mean either license applies — check what license actually accompanies that specific Project.

The Licensee CAN:

- use the Project;
- use it commercially;
- deploy it;
- modify it;
- redesign it;
- rename it;
- rebrand it;
- change the UI;
- change the functionality;
- connect it to another backend;
- connect APIs and services;
- hire another developer;
- maintain it independently;
- create private development copies;
- create reasonable backups;
- create modified versions for its own permitted use;
- remove LaLibertia branding unless otherwise agreed; and
- continue using the Project indefinitely while complying with the License.

The Licensee CANNOT, by default:

- resell the Source Code;
- sell the Project as a template;
- upload it as a downloadable source-code product;
- distribute the Figma/design files as a competing product;
- create a marketplace product from the Source Code;
- sublicense it to unrelated third parties;
- claim ownership of Third-Party Material;
- claim automatic exclusive ownership;
- claim automatic copyright assignment;
- distribute unrelated LaLibertia internal systems; or
- give another person broader rights than the Licensee received.

Simple rule:

> **Use it. Customize it. Deploy it. Build your business on it.**
>
> **Don't buy one copy and turn the underlying Source Code or Design Files into a product for selling to other people.**

